<<~ ahu #/head>>

! Canvas Shell UX ~ the canvas is primary, the chrome wraps it

''Nothing here names a vendor, because nothing here depends on one.'' Every law below holds for any DOM-hosting camera (#/wikispace-canvasspace-ontology).

''THE CORE LAW: the canvas is primary; UI wraps around it and never blocks it.''

The reference implementation studied is ''Kinopio'' (`kinopio.club`, Vue 3, open source, community-funded) — and a later field survey found the same house independently, as ''one of the category's few survivors'' and one of only two credible touch-first spatial tools. Two arrivals at one door is the cheapest evidence available that a design is load-bearing rather than a taste.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/pointer-events>>

!! The pointer-events discipline

Chrome sits at ''fixed viewport corners'' with `position: fixed; pointer-events: none`, and interaction is re-enabled ''only on the child controls that need it''. The canvas fills 100% of the viewport ''beneath'' it.

''Why this rather than a layout that reserves space:'' a shell that reserves space makes the canvas smaller, and on a small viewport the canvas is the scarce resource — measured elsewhere, an overview panel on a phone ''costs time and buys no preference'', because a larger detail view outweighs what the panel provides. Chrome that floats costs nothing but the pixels under it.

⚠ ''A canvas-mode toggle must exist.'' Dimming the shell (`opacity` down, `pointer-events: none`) and restoring the drawing surface's own tools is a mode, not a preference — freeform drawing and structured arrangement want different chrome, and a shell that cannot get out of the way will be fought.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-ios-viewport>>

!! ⚠ The iOS visual viewport ~ a fixed header is not fixed

''On iOS the software keyboard and pinch-zoom shrink the VISUAL viewport while leaving the LAYOUT viewport — and CSS `dvh` — unchanged.'' A `position: fixed` header therefore drifts off-screen or floats mid-canvas exactly when a user is typing or zooming.

The studied answer is to ''reposition the chrome against `visualViewport` on a frame cadence'' rather than trusting `fixed`. Android Chrome does not exhibit the same behaviour, so this is a platform branch and not a general rule.

⚠ Two adjacent traps, both measured and both live at WebKit:
* ''Scroll events fire only when scrolling FINISHES'' on iOS, so the keyboard's animation cannot be tracked — only its endpoints.
* ''An input with `font-size` under 16px makes iOS Safari zoom the page on focus.'' Set 16px minimum on every focusable control, or the camera moves ''because the user touched a text field''.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/one-finger-pan>>

!! One-finger pan ~ let the browser do it

''The single most valuable structural borrowing.'' Render the space as a ''real, page-sized DOM element'' and let the browser's ''native scroller'' perform one-finger pan. Momentum, rubber-banding and compositor-thread scrolling all arrive free, and none of them has to be reimplemented in JavaScript on the main thread.

''What it buys, measured against the alternative:'' the category's most-reported mobile blocker is that a user must switch tools to navigate and switch back to draw. A canvas that pans by native scroll never asks. Selection moves to long-press; two-finger tap and three-finger tap take undo and redo, matching the platform convention rather than inventing one.

⚠ ''This composes with the pointer-events discipline and NOT with a rasterised surface.'' A real DOM element under a real scroller is available only to a DOM-hosting camera — one more consequence of #/wikispace-canvasspace-ontology's surface correction, arriving from the mobile side.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/the-boundary-is-visible>>

!! A boundary the reader cannot see gets crossed by accident

''Where a thing sits on the durability ladder must read from the surface itself'', without a menu, a hover or a second click. Pending and promoted look different; a thing that will survive a reconnect looks different from a thing that will not.

This decorates nothing. Capability enforces the `canon-boundary` crossing, and people ''obey'' it when they can tell which side they work on. A reader who cannot see the line does not experience a refusal as protection — they experience it as the tool losing their work, and they route around it. ⚠ The visible distinction never ENFORCES anything: it makes the enforced line predictable, — the only thing a surface can honestly contribute to a rule the store already keeps.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/dual-load>>

!! Dual-load ~ the local replica renders first, the network merges after

''A place opens from local storage IMMEDIATELY and renders before reaching any peer.'' The transport connects in parallel and its deltas merge into what already stands on screen.

# open a place → restore the local snapshot → ''render at once''
# in parallel → connect → receive deltas → merge into live state
# on disconnect → ''keep editing'' → queue → replay on reconnect

''This states the local-first ideal as a shell behaviour'' — //no spinners: your work at your fingertips//, and //the network is optional//. A surface that waits for a peer before drawing has made the network a precondition, and a vessel whose canvas cannot open offline falls short of the sovereignty the rest of the stack holds.

⚠ ''The merge must not clear the surface.'' A remote delta arriving after first paint updates what stands; it never replaces the tree. A camera that re-renders wholesale on connect throws away the reader's caret, scroll and selection, — the very state the shell exists to keep.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/layout>>

!! The layout, surface-invariant

```
┌─ viewport (100vw × 100vh) ─────────────────────────────────┐
│  ┌─ HEADER  position:fixed  pointer-events:none ─────────┐ │
│  │  place · presence · share                             │ │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                                                            │
│  ┌─ CANVAS CAMERA  fills viewport, pans, zooms ──────────┐ │
│  │  meme frames · pranala wires · ahu boxes              │ │
│  │  portals · drop zones                                 │ │
│  │  remote cursors — canvas-relative, move with pan/zoom │ │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
│                                                            │
│  ┌─ FOOTER  position:fixed ──────────────────────────────┐ │
│  │  zoom% · place id · count · standing badge            │ │
│  └───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘ │
└────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
```

''Remote cursors are canvas-relative, never viewport-relative'' — they move with pan and zoom because they mark a place in the space, not a place on the glass.

<<~/ahu>>

